---
/*
 * /acceptable-use — the Acceptable Use Policy.
 *
 * WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE DOCUMENT AND NOT A SECTION OF THE TERMS. Three reasons, in order
 * of how much they matter; the third is the one that decided it:
 *
 *   1. IT CHANGES ON A DIFFERENT CLOCK. Abuse patterns move; contract terms should not. The
 *      Terms incorporate this document by reference and reserve the right to change it on
 *      its own, so a new abuse rule is a one-page edit rather than a re-issue of the whole
 *      agreement with a new effective date and a fresh acceptance question.
 *   2. IT IS THE DOCUMENT YOU SEND SOMEONE. When an account is cut off, the operator needs
 *      one short URL that says exactly which line was crossed. Section 7(c) of a contract is
 *      not that, and neither is a 20-section page the reader has to search.
 *   3. IT IS THE ONE A USER MIGHT ACTUALLY READ. It is short and it is about them. Burying
 *      it inside the Terms guarantees it is never read, which costs us the argument that the
 *      user was on notice — the only thing this document is really for.
 *
 * ENFORCEABILITY NOTE, so nobody softens it by accident: this document is deliberately
 * written as a list of things the OPERATOR MAY DO in response, not as a list of promises to
 * act. "We may" throughout, never "we will". A stated commitment to police the service is a
 * duty a plaintiff can allege we breached; a reserved discretion is not.
 *
 * THE NUMBERS ARE READ OUT OF THE CODE, NOT ASSUMED:
 *   3 live workspaces per verified identity -> FREE_TIER_WORKSPACE_LIMIT,
 *                                              supabase/functions/command/index.ts:330
 *                                              (the query excludes archived_at rows, but
 *                                              no product command archives a workspace)
 *   120 signals / credential / hour         -> SIGNAL_CREDENTIAL_LIMIT, :297
 *   1000 signals / workspace / hour         -> SIGNAL_WORKSPACE_LIMIT, :298
 *   hourly window, not rolling              -> incrementSignalBucket, :2342 uses
 *                                              date_trunc('hour', ...)
 *   body 1-2000, about <= 500, until <= 30d -> migrations/20260724000003_signals.sql:9-15
 *   invitation TTL <= 7 days                -> INVITATION_MAX_TTL_MS, :289
 *   agent credential TTL <= 8 hours         -> AGENT_TOKEN_MAX_TTL_MS, :290
 * If any of these change in the code, they change here. A published cap that no longer
 * matches the enforced one is worse than no published cap.
 *
 * NOTES GO IN THIS BLOCK, NEVER IN THE MARKUP — Astro ships <!-- comments --> verbatim.
 */

import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import LegalDoc, { type Section } from "../components/legal/LegalDoc.astro";

const EFFECTIVE = "27 July 2026";
const UPDATED = "29 July 2026";

const sections: Section[] = [
